// TEST: ML Perm Former Extent Rejected (rejects)
import ml as ml
// ============================================================================
// THE SECOND FALSE ACCEPT ON THE FORMER ARM — not the one 066 pins.
//
// 066 is about a former whose SOURCE is node-covariant; the sources are judged
// now and that is caught. This one has no covariant source at all, and it was
// still not a theorem: the arm answered `Pow 0` — THE INVARIANT STATUS —
// without ever asking how many cells the result has.
//
// `Pow 0` is a claim, not a shape: it says the value is FIXED by every node
// relabelling. For an aggregate that holds only when its cell count stays
// OUTSIDE the node-power space. Inside it, the S_n-fixed vectors are exactly
// the ones with every cell equal — which is why the literal arm already
// refuses `[0.0, 1.0, 2.0, 3.0]` at N = 4 and points at ml.derive_perm_bias,
// the op whose whole job is the equivariant constants.
//
// The former below builds that same 4-cell constant, cell by cell, out of the
// node index set. It is the literal, laundered through an iteration:
//
//     method_for(range<Idx<4>>) <@> lambda(i) -> 1.0 * i    ==   [0, 1, 2, 3]
//
// so `x + c` is certified S_4-equivariant while being the literal program the
// arm one line up refuses. Relabelling the nodes permutes x's cells but leaves
// c's alone, so the sum does not co-permute:
//
//   x = [10, 20, 30, 40]           -> x + c = [10, 21, 32, 43]
//   x = [20, 10, 30, 40] (0<->1)   -> x + c = [20, 11, 32, 43]
//
// S_4-equivariance would require the second to be the first with cells 0 and 1
// swapped, i.e. [21, 10, 32, 43]. It is not.
//
// Two guards now stand between that program and a certificate, and the test
// exercises both: `range<Idx<4>>` is classified by its EXTENT (4 = 4^1 lands
// in the node-power space, and the identity index array is fixed by no
// relabelling but the identity), and a former result inherits `Pow 0` only
// from a single source that is itself proven fixed. BL4012.
// ============================================================================
function bad(x: Array<Float like Idx<4>>) where ml.perm_equiv(4) -> Array<Float like Idx<4>> = {
    let c = method_for(range<Idx<4>>) <@> lambda(i) -> 1.0 * i |> compute
    x + c
}
